Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether $L^p(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^q(\mathbb{R}) \subseteq L^r(\mathbb{R})$ forces $r \in [p,q]$.

**Answer leak check:** The problem states only the known forward direction and asks about the converse. No solution or answer is leaked. Proceeding.

---

## Proof

We show the converse **holds**: if $p < q$ and $L^p(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^q(\mathbb{R}) \subseteq L^r(\mathbb{R})$, then $r \in [p,q]$.

We prove the contrapositive: if $r \notin [p,q]$, we construct $f \in L^p \cap L^q$ with $f \notin L^r$.

**Preliminary facts.** For $\gamma \in \mathbb{R}$:
- $\displaystyle\int_1^{\infty} x^{-\gamma}\,dx < \infty \iff \gamma > 1$ (behavior at infinity),
- $\displaystyle\int_0^{1} x^{-\gamma}\,dx < \infty \iff \gamma < 1$ (singularity at origin).

Consequently, for $f_s(x) = |x|^{-\alpha}\mathbf{1}_{|x|\ge 1}$: $f_s \in L^s \iff \alpha s > 1 \iff \alpha > 1/s$.  
For $g_s(x) = |x|^{-\beta}\mathbf{1}_{|x|\le 1}$: $g_s \in L^s \iff \beta s < 1 \iff \beta < 1/s$.

---

### Case 1: $r > q$ (failure via singularity at the origin)

Since $r > q > p \ge 1$, we have $\frac{1}{r} < \frac{1}{q} < \frac{1}{p}$. Choose
$$\beta \in \left[\frac{1}{r},\, \frac{1}{q}\right),$$
which is nonempty since $\frac{1}{r} < \frac{1}{q}$. Set $f(x) = |x|^{-\beta}\mathbf{1}_{|x|\le 1}$.

- **$f \in L^p$:** $\beta < \frac{1}{q} < \frac{1}{p}$, so $\beta p < 1$. ✓
- **$f \in L^q$:** $\beta < \frac{1}{q}$, so $\beta q < 1$. ✓
- **$f \notin L^r$:** $\beta \ge \frac{1}{r}$, so $\beta r \ge 1$, giving $\displaystyle\int_{|x|\le 1}|x|^{-\beta r}dx = \infty$. ✓

---

### Case 2: $r < p$ (failure via slow decay at infinity)

Since $r < p < q$, we have $\frac{1}{r} > \frac{1}{p} > \frac{1}{q}$. Choose
$$\alpha \in \left(\frac{1}{p},\, \frac{1}{r}\right],$$
which is nonempty since $\frac{1}{r} > \frac{1}{p}$. Set $f(x) = |x|^{-\alpha}\mathbf{1}_{|x|\ge 1}$.

- **$f \in L^p$:** $\alpha > \frac{1}{p}$, so $\alpha p > 1$. ✓
- **$f \in L^q$:** $\alpha > \frac{1}{p} > \frac{1}{q}$, so $\alpha q > 1$. ✓
- **$f \notin L^r$:** $\alpha \le \frac{1}{r}$, so $\alpha r \le 1$, giving $\displaystyle\int_{|x|\ge 1}|x|^{-\alpha r}dx = \infty$. ✓

---

### Conclusion

In both cases ($r > q$ and $r < p$), we exhibited a function in $L^p(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^q(\mathbb{R})$ that is **not** in $L^r(\mathbb{R})$. Therefore the inclusion $L^p \cap L^q \subseteq L^r$ can hold only when $r \in [p, q]$.

**The converse statement holds: $r \in [p,q]$ is necessary.** $\blacksquare$

